ZyperOS: indexed draws give OpenGL their index range (glDrawRangeElements),
so the driver does not read every draw's indices back to find it.

PPSSPP's OpenGL backend draws each flush with glDrawElements from its index
push buffer. Mesa's Panfrost (the Pixel 2's Mali-G31) needs the lowest and
highest index of every indexed draw to size its vertex job; without a range
from the program it looks the draw up in a small per-buffer cache, which every
write to the buffer clears, and PPSSPP writes fresh indices at a new offset
for every draw. So every draw missed it, and Panfrost mapped the index buffer
and scanned it on the CPU, from uncached GPU memory
(u_vbuf_get_minmax_index_mapped, panfrost_get_index_buffer_bounded in
pan_helpers.c). Sampled on the GL thread on a GKD Pixel 2: 52% of it in
Daxter's 3D title screen, 21% in God of War: Chains of Olympus's main menu.

The hardware-transform path already knows the range: its indices point into
the vertices DecodeVerts just decoded, numDecodedVerts_ of them, which
DecodeIndsAndGetData returns as maxIndex. DrawIndexed now takes the highest
index (-1, the default, for a caller that does not know it) and the queue
runner draws with glDrawRangeElements(mode, 0, maxIndex, ...) when it is known,
on desktop OpenGL or OpenGL ES 3. The software-transform path and thin3d's
draws are unchanged. The same pixels: the range only says where the indices
already are.

On the Pixel 2, the same screens, the core before and after (px2-perf, 20 s):
Daxter's title 23.5 to 30.0 frames a second (its own 30; the GL thread from
108% of a core to 58%), God of War's main menu 16.6 to 20.4 (its GPU is now
89% busy; the GL thread waits on the GPU, PANFROST_WAIT_BO, a fifth of its
time). No GPU fault in the kernel's log.

Upstream: any Mesa driver that needs the range pays the same scan for
PPSSPP's streamed indices; Panfrost is one.

